109
00:07:56,580 --> 00:07:57,782
Rym: There is some other news today.

110
00:07:58,123 --> 00:07:59,485
Rym: It's very GeekNights news.

111
00:07:59,906 --> 00:08:08,260
Rym: If you don't know, jaywalking in the United States, but particularly in New York City, wasn't always a crime.

112
00:08:09,742 --> 00:08:15,220
Rym: And most of the reasons why it became a crime were, surprise, extremely racist.

113
00:08:16,280 --> 00:08:18,665
Scott: Yeah, well, I mean, cars didn't exist, right?

114
00:08:18,786 --> 00:08:21,211
Scott: But roads existed before cars.

115
00:08:21,532 --> 00:08:25,060
Scott: I don't know if people realize that, that roads predate cars.

116
00:08:25,680 --> 00:08:27,704
Scott: So, you know, even Romans had roads.

117
00:08:28,044 --> 00:08:28,846
Scott: They didn't have cars.

118
00:08:29,427 --> 00:08:36,220
Scott: And so when you're on a road, before cars existed, everything that could go on a road was relatively slow.

119
00:08:36,961 --> 00:08:46,460
Scott: And if you really wouldn't, if you had a horse, which is pretty much the fastest thing you could get, you wouldn't go quickly on a road with it unless, you know, especially in a crowded area.

120
00:08:46,700 --> 00:08:52,080
Scott: You know, maybe out in the woods, you know, to get from town to town, you might speed up a little bit, but also a horse is going to get tired.

121
00:08:53,041 --> 00:08:55,569
Scott: And if a horse, you know, sees a person in front of it, it's an animal.

122
00:08:55,589 --> 00:08:57,213
Scott: It's going to react on its own.

123
00:08:57,254 --> 00:08:59,340
Scott: You don't have to worry about the human reaction time necessarily.

124
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:01,825
Scott: A horse could run a person over too.

125
00:09:01,845 --> 00:09:03,368
Scott: That can happen.

126
00:09:04,109 --> 00:09:09,360
Scott: But you didn't have horses running in the city, like at horse speed, like as the Kentucky Derby, right?

127
00:09:09,780 --> 00:09:26,500
Rym: Well, one time we did, in fact, President Grant was pulled over by a constable for recklessly riding his horse at speed through the streets of DC one day and issued a ticket by a cop who did not know he was the president.

128
00:09:27,963 --> 00:09:29,249
Scott: I mean, good.

129
00:09:29,852 --> 00:09:31,540
Scott: I would issue a ticket even if he was the president.

130
00:09:31,600 --> 00:09:35,239
Rym: From what I read, he paid it and was basically like, yeah, like, good on you.

131
00:09:37,421 --> 00:09:37,581
Scott: Yeah.

132
00:09:37,641 --> 00:09:44,094
Scott: But anyway, the roads before cars were just kind of a free for all because they kind of could be.

133
00:09:44,114 --> 00:09:47,160
Scott: It wasn't really a problem too much.

134
00:09:47,380 --> 00:09:49,004
Scott: I mean, you know, there were some problems.

135
00:09:49,265 --> 00:09:53,656
Scott: You know, it's not like nobody ever got run over by a wagon, but it wasn't a crazy problem.

136
00:09:55,360 --> 00:09:58,165
Rym: As cities got denser, it did become more and more of a crazy problem.

137
00:09:58,806 --> 00:10:07,320
Rym: There's a street in New York City that was referred to in the late 1800s as the Avenue of Death because of how many kids were crushed by horse carts and trains during that era.

138
00:10:08,002 --> 00:10:13,960
Scott: No, trains are a different, you know, but but like there wasn't any rules about what you could go on in the street, right?

139
00:10:14,060 --> 00:10:16,408
Scott: It's like kids could just go out in the street and start playing stickball.

140
00:10:16,448 --> 00:10:16,990
Scott: No problem.

141
00:10:17,150 --> 00:10:17,351
Scott: Right.

142
00:10:17,371 --> 00:10:17,732
Scott: It wasn't.

143
00:10:18,053 --> 00:10:19,036
Scott: you didn't need like a permit.

144
00:10:19,076 --> 00:10:20,180
Scott: You didn't close the road.

145
00:10:20,581 --> 00:10:26,956
Scott: You could go set up, you know, pretty much, you know, have your cart with your with your wares for sale just out in the street.

146
00:10:27,417 --> 00:10:28,560
Scott: And it was totally fine.

147
00:10:28,660 --> 00:10:34,620
Rym: Streets were part of the fabric of communal spaces in urban places in America.

148
00:10:35,001 --> 00:10:39,212
Scott: It was basically just a public place where everyone could come out and do, you know, it was.

149
00:10:39,252 --> 00:10:42,560
Scott: it was for transportation, but it was also a plaza at the same time.

150
00:10:42,660 --> 00:10:45,990
Scott: It was just the space that's, you know, shared between everybody.

151
00:10:46,031 --> 00:10:48,919
Scott: Come out and see people and then your space is in your house.

152
00:10:50,421 --> 00:10:56,910
Scott: And so when cars came about, cars had nowhere to drive except on the road and they just sort of was.

153
00:10:56,970 --> 00:11:03,220
Scott: there were lots of battles and then cars due to, you know, many evil reasons and money and whatnot.

154
00:11:05,001 --> 00:11:11,699
Scott: Just kind of won and just conquered the road and made the road only for transportation and storage of car.

155
00:11:12,720 --> 00:11:14,926
Scott: And everyone else had to just get out the way.

156
00:11:15,046 --> 00:11:17,733
Scott: And part of that was, you know, they made it up.

157
00:11:17,814 --> 00:11:19,017
Scott: They made up jaywalking.

158
00:11:19,037 --> 00:11:20,200
Scott: It was like an invented thing.

159
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:24,819
Rym: The term jaywalking used to basically refer to actually read the etymology of this.

160
00:11:26,020 --> 00:11:32,280
Rym: Jay was a derogative term for country bumpkins who wander around a city and don't know what the fuck they're doing and get in everyone's way.

161
00:11:32,642 --> 00:11:36,240
Rym: So jaywalking was walking around like an idiot in the city.

162
00:11:37,861 --> 00:11:42,360
Rym: But jaywalking became the crime of daring to be in the way of the cars.

163
00:11:43,900 --> 00:11:47,406
Scott: And even worse, jaywalking became, you know, they sort of tilted the script, right?

164
00:11:47,426 --> 00:11:56,100
Scott: The original script was like, hey, this is the space for everybody and you're coming in with your car acting like this space that's for everybody is just for you to get where you're going.

165
00:11:56,220 --> 00:11:57,163
Scott: But it's not.

166
00:11:57,203 --> 00:11:58,988
Scott: That's only one of the uses of this space.

167
00:11:59,430 --> 00:12:01,736
Scott: And then cars said, no, this space belongs to us.

168
00:12:01,797 --> 00:12:02,940
Scott: And they won that battle.

169
00:12:04,161 --> 00:12:06,226
Scott: And then people stopped fighting it and took it for granted.

170
00:12:07,349 --> 00:12:11,520
Scott: And now we, you know, smart people have realized that, hey, that was wrong.

171
00:12:12,302 --> 00:12:13,627
Scott: And I'll give you a mistake.

172
00:12:13,908 --> 00:12:16,880
Rym: The reason we were able to do this in New York City, what just happened.

173
00:12:17,040 --> 00:12:18,385
Rym: Jaywalking is no longer a crime.

174
00:12:18,666 --> 00:12:20,332
Rym: It's not against the law in New York City.

175
00:12:20,573 --> 00:12:21,236
Rym: Full stop.

176
00:12:21,577 --> 00:12:22,320
Rym: Period at the end.

177
00:12:22,560 --> 00:12:25,405
Rym: You're legally allowed to cross the street anywhere you want.

178
00:12:25,666 --> 00:12:27,469
Rym: You don't have to walk in crosswalks.

179
00:12:27,689 --> 00:12:34,140
Rym: You can basically legally, legally is the important word here, cross the street anywhere.

180
00:12:35,060 --> 00:12:39,199
Rym: Practically, you should generally avoid the cars hitting you.

181
00:12:39,980 --> 00:12:40,121
Scott: Right.

182
00:12:40,141 --> 00:12:48,560
Scott: So the thing is, even though it was made a crime to be like a pro car thing, it was never really, you know, I don't think at any point in time was it super strictly enforced.

183
00:12:49,282 --> 00:12:50,928
Rym: Ah, that's why it was overturned.

184
00:12:51,028 --> 00:12:54,258
Rym: So it was being enforced in New York City.

185
00:12:54,900 --> 00:12:55,622
Scott: Yeah.

186
00:12:55,642 --> 00:13:05,420
Scott: It wasn't strictly enforced at like any point where it was like, it was never a case where like everyone who jaywalked was getting jaywalking tickets constantly and they were making sure they were trying to get people out of the road.

187
00:13:05,961 --> 00:13:10,800
Rym: It was always just feel like it is like it's weird being in a city where people don't jaywalk.

188
00:13:11,421 --> 00:13:22,260
Scott: But the point is that the law was mostly throughout its history only used by cops to harass people who they wanted to harass, right?

189
00:13:22,500 --> 00:13:27,508
Scott: Like if a rich white dude walked out into the road, you know, the cop wasn't going to do anything.

190
00:13:27,548 --> 00:13:34,820
Scott: But if he saw a black person walk out in the road, the cop could be like, aha, I now have an excuse to go fuck with you because I'm a racist piece of shit cop.

191
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:39,160
Scott: And that's even today how that law was basically being used.

192
00:13:39,380 --> 00:13:39,601
Rym: Yep.

193
00:13:39,661 --> 00:13:49,220
Rym: So last year in twenty twenty three, more than 90 percent, 90 percent of all jaywalking tickets written in New York City were written to black and Latino people.

194
00:13:49,982 --> 00:13:55,040
Scott: Even though literally every single person who walks in New York City is jaywalking.

195
00:13:55,400 --> 00:13:58,453
Rym: I jaywalk on average more than 10 times a day.

196
00:13:58,514 --> 00:14:00,000
Rym: And that's probably a low estimate.

197
00:14:00,581 --> 00:14:00,762
Scott: Right.

198
00:14:00,802 --> 00:14:01,965
Scott: The cops were only.

199
00:14:01,985 --> 00:14:07,820
Scott: they weren't using this law on tourists who were walking when the crossing light was was red at the crosswalk.

200
00:14:08,340 --> 00:14:12,270
Scott: They weren't using it on, you know, people running across.

201
00:14:12,611 --> 00:14:12,812
Scott: Right.

202
00:14:12,852 --> 00:14:15,860
Scott: They were not using even though all those people were jaywalking.

203
00:14:16,841 --> 00:14:18,286
Scott: None of them were getting in trouble.

204
00:14:18,326 --> 00:14:22,320
Scott: The cops are using it on black and Latino people because they're racist.

205
00:14:22,480 --> 00:14:30,860
Rym: Mercedes Narcisse, our city council member from Brooklyn, who sponsored this, a Democrat, obviously, she has a great quote when asked for comment.

206
00:14:31,220 --> 00:14:31,903
Rym: Let's be real.

207
00:14:32,285 --> 00:14:33,731
Rym: Every New Yorker jaywalks.

208
00:14:33,972 --> 00:14:35,700
Rym: People are simply trying to get where they need to go.

209
00:14:36,080 --> 00:14:41,979
Rym: Laws that penalize common behaviors for everyday movement shouldn't exist, especially when they unfairly impact communities of color.

210
00:14:43,580 --> 00:14:51,360
Rym: So this is like kind of a big deal, but I think it's going to take a while for this to make things different in New York City because...

211
00:14:51,500 --> 00:14:51,861
Scott: I don't think.

212
00:14:52,082 --> 00:14:53,166
Scott: But the thing is, it doesn't.

213
00:14:53,226 --> 00:14:56,860
Scott: The real problem, right, is, you know, obviously the law was a problem.

214
00:14:57,080 --> 00:14:57,641
Scott: They should go away.

215
00:14:57,742 --> 00:15:05,260
Scott: But it's like, hey, maybe you should mention the fact that, like, you know, this law could have been used on white people.

216
00:15:05,581 --> 00:15:07,911
Scott: It's like the cops are still racist as fuck.

217
00:15:07,931 --> 00:15:09,156
Scott: And this does nothing to change that.

218
00:15:09,236 --> 00:15:09,999
Scott: That's the problem.

219
00:15:10,700 --> 00:15:11,507
Rym: That's a problem.

220
00:15:11,748 --> 00:15:13,180
Rym: Also, the problem that we've sacrificed.

221
00:15:13,420 --> 00:15:19,840
Scott: Why don't you go through and find every cop who wrote a jaywalking ticket and fire them and never allow them to be a cop again everywhere, anywhere.

222
00:15:20,843 --> 00:15:21,998
Scott: Because clearly they're racist.

223
00:15:23,020 --> 00:15:23,701
Rym: A hundred percent.

224
00:15:25,044 --> 00:15:34,040
Rym: But I think independent of that, over time, activists will be able to use this to increasingly reclaim streets.

225
00:15:34,520 --> 00:15:43,920
Rym: But it's going to be a slow roll because even if it is technically not against the law anymore, practically it's a lot like how in New York you can go outside without a shirt on.

226
00:15:44,120 --> 00:15:45,348
Rym: Man, woman, whatever, doesn't matter.

227
00:15:45,368 --> 00:15:46,495
Rym: Like, you're allowed to be shirtless.

228
00:15:46,515 --> 00:15:47,280
Rym: That's fully legal.

229
00:15:47,721 --> 00:15:55,400
Rym: But at the same time, if you, say, are female presenting and you go out without a shirt on, people are going to be a problem around you.

230
00:15:55,580 --> 00:16:00,079
Rym: Just like if you just walk out into the street, a car might just hit you.

231
00:16:00,600 --> 00:16:04,820
Rym: So this will require a critical mass of people actively knowing about this.

232
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:20,140
Rym: And I think the short-term thing activists can do, especially biking activists and pedestrian activists, is spread the word about this and start to try to encourage, over time, people basically reclaiming streets by crossing en masse and getting in the way of the cars, especially in Manhattan.

233
00:16:23,589 --> 00:16:24,696
Scott: Well, it's gone now.

234
00:16:24,898 --> 00:16:25,220
Scott: Bye-bye.

235
00:16:26,689 --> 00:16:27,139
Scott: At least here.

236
00:16:28,466 --> 00:16:30,220
Scott: I don't know what the jaywalking laws are elsewhere.

237
00:16:30,840 --> 00:16:35,600
Scott: Is there somewhere else on earth where jaywalking is enforced?

238
00:16:36,627 --> 00:16:37,800
Rym: I don't jaywalk in Singapore.

239
00:16:39,521 --> 00:16:41,140
Scott: Is there cops on every corner?

240
00:16:42,103 --> 00:16:43,654
Rym: Uh, kinda.

241
00:16:43,815 --> 00:16:44,359
Rym: In the CVD.

242
00:16:45,502 --> 00:16:45,783
Scott: I don't know.

243
00:16:45,843 --> 00:16:48,960
Scott: It's like, no matter what, anyone who jaywalks will just get busted.

244
00:16:49,280 --> 00:16:53,900
Rym: I do not break any law, no matter how minor, when I'm in Singapore.

245
00:16:54,220 --> 00:16:55,209
Scott: What is their jaywalking?

246
00:16:55,310 --> 00:16:56,380
Scott: Do they have a jaywalking law?

247
00:16:56,440 --> 00:16:57,146
Rym: I can look it up.

248
00:16:57,448 --> 00:16:58,918
Rym: I'm pretty sure they do.

249
00:17:01,102 --> 00:17:03,538
Rym: I haven't been there in a while, but I'm going to be there.

250
00:17:04,500 --> 00:17:05,306
Rym: Is jaywalking enforced?

251
00:17:05,346 --> 00:17:05,426
Rym: Yes.

252
00:17:08,632 --> 00:17:08,819
Rym: Alright.

253
00:17:09,740 --> 00:17:15,630
Rym: It's selectively enforced, but it is enforced, et cetera.

254
00:17:15,951 --> 00:17:17,390
Scott: You don't see people jaywalking there?

255
00:17:17,589 --> 00:17:21,429
Rym: The fine for smoking outside is an order of magnitude higher than the fine for jaywalking.

256
00:17:21,711 --> 00:17:21,811
Rym: Good.

257
00:17:21,893 --> 00:17:22,826
Scott: Well, that should be it, right?

258
00:17:23,596 --> 00:17:24,047
Rym: It should be.

259
00:17:26,090 --> 00:17:31,070
Scott: If only we could enforce, you know, but that enforcement isn't even the answer for that, though.

260
00:17:31,170 --> 00:17:32,930
Scott: The answer is, stop selling.

261
00:17:34,996 --> 00:17:35,409
Scott: Stop asking.

262
00:17:37,031 --> 00:17:39,770
Rym: But anyway, things of the day.

263
00:17:40,851 --> 00:17:45,249
Rym: This thing of the day, I just bumped into it, and I think I have now hit the peak.

264
00:17:46,711 --> 00:17:49,389
Rym: Principal Skinner, Steamed Hands mashup.

265
00:17:50,730 --> 00:17:54,730
Rym: Meghalat Hamed, The Scroll of the Steamed Portions of Sham.

266
00:17:56,911 --> 00:17:58,055
Rym: Just click on it.

267
00:17:58,577 --> 00:18:01,690
Rym: If you are Jewish, or know Jewish people, you will get the joke.

268
00:18:02,973 --> 00:18:04,949
Rym: Or you'll at least recognize what's going on here.

269
00:18:06,371 --> 00:18:12,149
Rym: If you don't know what this is, I highly advise you to talk to one of your Jewish friends and ask them what they think of this.

270
00:18:12,950 --> 00:18:20,690
Scott: I'm the Jewish friend, so basically what's going on here is, you know, if you know the Hebrew language or not, they're obviously singing in Hebrew.

271
00:18:21,671 --> 00:18:28,489
Scott: If you go to any Jewish religious service, you know, especially on the high holidays, there's a person who's called the cantor.

272
00:18:29,612 --> 00:18:38,290
Scott: And if you know Latin, or it's like in Spanish, what's the word for the verb to sing is like cantar, right?

273
00:18:38,350 --> 00:18:40,238
Scott: It's the same root, generally.

274
00:18:40,258 --> 00:18:42,770
Scott: It's basically the singing person, right?

275
00:18:43,410 --> 00:18:49,028
Scott: Some places have, you know, big synagogues will have a permanent person who's their guy or girl, whatever.

276
00:18:50,833 --> 00:18:53,050
Scott: Other places will like hire one just for the holidays.

277
00:18:53,810 --> 00:18:58,650
Scott: And, you know, they come in because otherwise it's like just reading prayers is boring.

278
00:18:59,410 --> 00:19:02,389
Rym: Oh yeah, someone who grew up going to a Catholic church off and on, yeah.

279
00:19:03,871 --> 00:19:08,190
Scott: So, you know, they get someone in there to sing, right?

280
00:19:08,831 --> 00:19:25,410
Scott: And so they're singing basically what Skinner and Chalmers, you know, the lyrics, the script of the steamed hams, right, has been adapted into lyrics and translated to Hebrew.

281
00:19:25,910 --> 00:19:32,510
Scott: And then they are singing it in the style of a cantor at, you know, a Jewish religious service.

282
00:19:33,991 --> 00:19:37,420
Scott: I don't know if it's a particular song that they've got the tune to.

283
00:19:37,480 --> 00:19:41,230
Scott: It's not a song I'm familiar with, so there may be a deeper level that I don't know.

284
00:19:41,831 --> 00:19:45,430
Scott: But I do know quite a few of those songs, and this isn't one of the ones I know.

285
00:19:45,450 --> 00:19:47,436
Rym: This person appears to do this for works.

286
00:19:47,677 --> 00:19:48,881
Rym: It's kind of a thing they do.

287
00:19:49,984 --> 00:19:51,690
Rym: They seem to be very knowledgeable.

288
00:19:52,711 --> 00:19:56,830
Rym: But also, the YouTube comments are actually sublime.

289
00:19:58,050 --> 00:20:01,937
Rym: Translators note, the actual dish served in the scroll is unclear.

290
00:20:02,178 --> 00:20:08,850
Rym: Modern translators tend variously toward clams or hams, despite neither being acceptable under contemporary Jewish law.

291
00:20:09,530 --> 00:20:10,393
Rym: A pillar of flame?

292
00:20:10,413 --> 00:20:16,670
Rym: At this time of year, at this time of day, in this part of Canaan, localized entirely within the tabernacle?

293
00:20:19,071 --> 00:20:20,955
Rym: Every comment here is solid gold.

294
00:20:21,095 --> 00:20:22,618
Rym: I'm very happy about this.

295
00:20:22,638 --> 00:20:25,444
Rym: It's also nine minutes long, which is an impressive effort.

296
00:20:25,464 --> 00:20:28,310
Rym: So what do you got?

297
00:20:30,711 --> 00:20:38,810
Scott: So, you know, somebody asked the question, right, what is the first American graphic novel?

298
00:20:38,930 --> 00:20:42,120
Scott: Now, this is largely a semantic question, right?

299
00:20:42,160 --> 00:20:43,364
Scott: What counts as American?

300
00:20:43,925 --> 00:20:45,390
Scott: What counts as a graphic novel?

301
00:20:46,751 --> 00:20:50,958
Scott: First, I think we can agree on what first means, right?

302
00:20:51,198 --> 00:20:58,170
Scott: But even if you come down to first, it's like, well, does it count as first if it wasn't published by a big publisher?

303
00:20:58,370 --> 00:21:01,638
Scott: If it was just something someone scrawled, right?

304
00:21:01,679 --> 00:21:02,340
Scott: That kind of thing.

305
00:21:02,420 --> 00:21:06,110
Scott: So this person makes this semantic argument, and you know what?

306
00:21:06,170 --> 00:21:09,260
Scott: I don't really agree with their semantic argument very much at all.

307
00:21:10,243 --> 00:21:12,149
Scott: I don't like semantic arguments to begin with.

308
00:21:12,711 --> 00:21:19,910
Scott: But in this case, even though this person I disagree with rather somewhat strongly, it's okay.

309
00:21:20,411 --> 00:21:26,323
Scott: Because what they do is they go through every single one throughout history.

310
00:21:26,584 --> 00:21:28,888
Scott: that might count, right?

311
00:21:28,928 --> 00:21:29,650
Scott: Chronologically.

312
00:21:29,950 --> 00:21:36,530
Scott: And then you can just decide for yourself which one you think is a graphic novel, is American, and is first.

313
00:21:39,691 --> 00:21:41,312
Scott: Or whatever your criteria is.

314
00:21:41,373 --> 00:21:42,594
Scott: Or does that even matter, right?

315
00:21:42,614 --> 00:22:03,170
Scott: What matters, though, even if you don't pick a winner, so to say, or engage with the semantic argument at any level, you can still learn the history of, right, you know, illustrated graphic storytelling in the United States of America with this article going way back.

316
00:22:03,591 --> 00:22:12,870
Scott: And in that sense, this is a terrific article because they researched that aspect as well as I've seen anyone, you know, research it, right?

317
00:22:13,030 --> 00:22:18,970
Rym: I mean, I only like to engage in a semantic argument for fun or in the pursuit of specific academia.

318
00:22:21,291 --> 00:22:24,199
Scott: For, you know, sometimes you just can't communicate with a person.

319
00:22:24,239 --> 00:22:28,290
Scott: Like, they don't understand what you mean, and then you need to agree on what a word means.

320
00:22:29,171 --> 00:22:31,375
Scott: That's usually a problem we have at work.

321
00:22:32,897 --> 00:22:35,401
Scott: It's like the English doesn't have enough words.

322
00:22:35,621 --> 00:22:39,848
Scott: So we're like, okay, we got the config and we got the settings and we have the...

323
00:22:41,470 --> 00:22:48,410
Rym: Yeah, as a VR project I'm working on with Emily, what's a scene versus a shot versus a scenario versus a story?

324
00:22:49,551 --> 00:22:56,504
Scott: And you run out of words real fast and you start need to, you know, which one, which config are you talking about?

325
00:22:57,086 --> 00:22:58,368
Scott: That one, which one?

326
00:22:58,508 --> 00:22:58,849
Scott: Oh, that.

327
00:22:58,929 --> 00:22:59,230
Scott: Okay.

328
00:23:03,011 --> 00:23:09,230
Rym: In the meta moment, Geek Night will be live at PAX Unplugged, which is coming up in December.

329
00:23:09,731 --> 00:23:14,530
Rym: We're going to be there on Friday doing a learn and play of an expansion to Deep Sea Adventure.

330
00:23:14,630 --> 00:23:19,390
Rym: We're also going to be doing an updated version of an old panel titled Take Your Fucking Turn.

331
00:23:20,091 --> 00:23:25,170
Rym: I will say that if you are a fan of GeekNights, you don't need to go to our panel because you're not going to learn anything new.

332
00:23:25,510 --> 00:23:40,870
Rym: That panel is specifically aimed at all the people at Unplugged who may not know who we are, or more importantly, trying to fill a panel room with people who will see a panel titled Take Your Fucking Turn at a board game convention and want to go see what the deal is, thus filling a panel room and getting them out of the crowd in the hallway.

333
00:23:42,731 --> 00:23:47,101
Rym: But the learn and play, like, if you've never played Deep Sea Adventure, we reviewed it on GeekNights a while ago.

334
00:23:47,121 --> 00:23:48,444
Rym: It's real fun.

335
00:23:48,765 --> 00:23:50,810
Rym: It's a real good push your luck game for what it is.

336
00:23:51,451 --> 00:23:56,050
Rym: And this is an expansion that adds slightly more luck pushing to that game.

337
00:23:59,222 --> 00:24:02,553
Rym: Otherwise, no GeekNights except maybe some Rym Specials for the next two weeks.

338
00:24:02,714 --> 00:24:04,420
Rym: I'm actually flying to London for a work thing.

339
00:24:05,840 --> 00:24:12,920
Rym: I'm just going to sidestep the fact that it's going to be real stressful and weird to not be in the country next week during the most painful election in my life.

340
00:24:13,340 --> 00:24:14,107
Rym: It may be safer.

341
00:24:14,127 --> 00:24:15,579
Rym: I don't think it'll be less stressful, though.

342
00:24:16,542 --> 00:24:16,827
Scott: Oh, no.

343
00:24:16,970 --> 00:24:17,378
Scott: Not at all.

344
00:24:18,941 --> 00:24:26,932
Rym: And then the following week, I'll be in Singapore, which, depending on how the previous week went, will either be jubilations or we'll see.

345
00:24:26,972 --> 00:24:30,337
Rym: We'll see what it's like to be in Singapore in the week after the election.

346
00:24:30,558 --> 00:24:31,859
Rym: That determines the fate of the next 40 years.

347
00:24:32,882 --> 00:24:33,539
Scott: Maybe stay there.

348
00:24:35,381 --> 00:24:40,470
Rym: I did get a request to interview for a job there recently, unrelatedly.

349
00:24:40,731 --> 00:24:43,856
Rym: They really like to hire people from the US to move to Singapore and take something over.

350
00:24:44,337 --> 00:24:45,800
Rym: Just generally, like all the companies there.

351
00:24:48,421 --> 00:24:51,269
Scott: My ancestors ran away from the country.

352
00:24:54,057 --> 00:24:55,180
Scott: It's not the worst strategy.

353
00:24:56,680 --> 00:24:57,691
Scott: You got to do what you got to do.

354
00:24:59,581 --> 00:25:05,712
Rym: The most important thing you can do in any bad situation as a human is survive and help the people you know survive.

355
00:25:06,172 --> 00:25:10,480
Rym: That's the basic stakes before you move on to solving other problems.

356
00:25:12,962 --> 00:25:16,289
Rym: So, yeah, probably no GeekNights except a couple of Rym specials for the next couple of weeks.

357
00:25:16,309 --> 00:25:18,413
Rym: And then we'll get back to hopefully business as usual.

358
00:25:18,434 --> 00:25:21,420
Rym: And I think we'll dive right into it because it's Halloween.

359
00:25:21,820 --> 00:25:23,688
Scott: Or business as very unusual.

360
00:25:24,994 --> 00:25:26,320
Scott: New harder business.

361
00:25:26,680 --> 00:25:27,001
Rym: Yeah.

362
00:25:27,401 --> 00:25:32,710
Rym: I don't think I need to tell anyone listening to this that you should vote for the Democrats and Kamala Harris.

363
00:25:32,931 --> 00:25:38,360
Rym: Like, if somehow that is not your plan, I don't know what to say at this point.

364
00:25:39,561 --> 00:25:43,128
Scott: Why are you listening to us or start listening to us?

365
00:25:45,693 --> 00:25:46,755
Scott: Who have you been listening to?

366
00:25:46,775 --> 00:25:49,360
Scott: Whoever you're listening to, stop.

367
00:25:49,841 --> 00:25:52,317
Rym: Stop listening to Joe Rogan or whatever it is you listen to.

368
00:25:54,031 --> 00:25:54,153
Rym: Yeah.

369
00:25:54,174 --> 00:25:54,419
Rym: Seriously.

370
00:25:55,882 --> 00:25:56,383
Rym: Yeah.

371
00:25:56,623 --> 00:25:57,224
Rym: But get right into it.

372
00:25:57,285 --> 00:25:59,469
Rym: It's Halloween.

373
00:25:59,469 --> 00:26:00,671
Rym: 19 years ago, we started GeekNights.

374
00:26:01,032 --> 00:26:05,140
Rym: So, in 2019, we reviewed It's the Great Pumpkin Charlie Brown, which I'm going to link to.

375
00:26:05,160 --> 00:26:06,000
Scott: I watched it yesterday.

376
00:26:06,564 --> 00:26:08,860
Rym: I watched parts of it again recently a few days ago.

377
00:26:08,900 --> 00:26:10,760
Scott: You know what I realized about Great Pumpkin Charlie Brown?

378
00:26:10,940 --> 00:26:11,539
Rym: What'd you realize?

379
00:26:12,843 --> 00:26:14,859
Scott: I hadn't watched it in a very, very long time.

380
00:26:15,241 --> 00:26:17,700
Rym: We did a show on it in 2019.

381
00:26:17,700 --> 00:26:17,941
Scott: Yeah.

382
00:26:18,042 --> 00:26:18,805
Scott: I don't remember that.

383
00:26:19,508 --> 00:26:22,240
Scott: But maybe I said the same thing in 2019.

384
00:26:22,240 --> 00:26:27,880
Rym: I'm going to read the description of this episode then to you, because I think we talked about what you're about to say.

385
00:26:28,420 --> 00:26:31,975
Rym: Tonight on GeekNights, we consider the 1996 Peanuts television special.

386
00:26:31,995 --> 00:26:33,120
Rym: It's the Great Pumpkin Charlie Brown.

387
00:26:33,480 --> 00:26:40,940
Rym: From an exploration of PTSD and the horrors of war to the inherent fanaticism of religion, it has some surprisingly nuanced elements.

388
00:26:41,620 --> 00:26:42,402
Scott: No, no, no, no.

389
00:26:42,462 --> 00:26:49,420
Scott: The thing that I was going to comment on has nothing to do with those things, which are very true, is the fact that, damn, it's really short.

390
00:26:50,034 --> 00:26:50,260
Scott: Oh, yeah.

391
00:26:50,881 --> 00:26:55,720
Scott: I thought it was longer, because when I was a kid, it was always full of ads, and they stretched it the fuck out.

392
00:26:55,960 --> 00:26:58,600
Scott: And if you just watch it without ads, it's real short.

393
00:26:58,940 --> 00:27:03,160
Rym: The GeekNights episode about it, even if you cut out the news and the things of the day, is longer than it.

394
00:27:06,525 --> 00:27:07,078
Scott: It's like...anyway.

395
00:27:07,843 --> 00:27:09,660
Rym: It is dense with content as well.

396
00:27:10,241 --> 00:27:22,020
Rym: We also, in October 25th, 2006, in the second year of GeekNights, we reviewed an anime that I'm sure 100% of you have forgotten, called Pumpkin Scissors.

397
00:27:22,560 --> 00:27:24,220
Rym: And I don't remember anything about that.

398
00:27:24,220 --> 00:27:26,720
Scott: I remember that there was an anime called Pumpkin Scissors.

399
00:27:26,780 --> 00:27:27,682
Scott: It was Gonzo.

400
00:27:28,944 --> 00:27:37,380
Rym: A lot of people compared it to Fullmetal Alchemist, but I'm just going to say it was pretty bad, because I never thought about it or talked about it again.

401
00:27:39,321 --> 00:27:46,180
Rym: But somehow, in all these years, we have never talked about carving pumpkins, which is a thing we have some opinions on and have engaged with.

402
00:27:46,180 --> 00:27:48,440
Scott: You didn't talk about it within a Halloween episode?

403
00:27:48,500 --> 00:27:49,524
Rym: Not that I can see.

404
00:27:49,544 --> 00:27:53,280
Rym: We talked about it adjacently in some Halloween episodes.

405
00:27:53,720 --> 00:28:03,020
Scott: Well, the first thing about carving pumpkins is that even though Halloween celebrations have spread worldwide, people may not realize pumpkins are not worldwide.

406
00:28:05,041 --> 00:28:09,653
Scott: You can't really grow a pumpkin if you're celebrating Halloween in, I don't know, Bermuda.

407
00:28:10,976 --> 00:28:12,099
Scott: Maybe you import a pumpkin?

408
00:28:12,179 --> 00:28:12,600
Scott: I don't know.

409
00:28:12,941 --> 00:28:15,020
Rym: I'm confident you can import a pumpkin by now.

410
00:28:15,680 --> 00:28:18,366
Scott: Maybe, but pumpkins don't grow everywhere.

411
00:28:18,426 --> 00:28:21,111
Scott: Whereas where we live, they grow like crazy.

412
00:28:21,152 --> 00:28:23,276
Scott: They're real easy to grow, as long as you've got land.

413
00:28:23,316 --> 00:28:25,120
Scott: If you've got land, you can grow pumpkins here.

414
00:28:26,325 --> 00:28:27,632
Rym: The U.S.

415
00:28:27,692 --> 00:28:29,240
Rym: is lousy with pumpkins.

416
00:28:30,982 --> 00:28:31,650
Scott: We've got way too many.

417
00:28:33,020 --> 00:28:40,360
Rym: In the modern world, one, two, three, four, five, six countries produce almost all the pumpkins that anyone uses in the world.

418
00:28:40,920 --> 00:28:41,248
Scott: Is U.S.

419
00:28:41,310 --> 00:28:41,720
Scott: number one?

420
00:28:41,880 --> 00:28:45,000
Rym: Number one is China, actually, by a huge margin.

421
00:28:45,220 --> 00:28:50,360
Scott: But are they growing the orange pumpkins for Halloween, or are they growing some other kind?

422
00:28:50,440 --> 00:28:51,306
Rym: Ah, it's Wikipedia.

423
00:28:51,346 --> 00:28:53,099
Rym: This includes squash and gourds.

424
00:28:53,960 --> 00:29:00,900
Scott: Yeah, I'm talking about specifically the orange pumpkin that you use on Halloween, not some other kind.

425
00:29:01,100 --> 00:29:11,680
Rym: Yeah, I don't know the name of the cultivar, but if you're not familiar with the American pumpkin that we're thinking of, it's big, it's orange, it tastes bad.

426
00:29:12,020 --> 00:29:15,460
Scott: I'm pretty sure they got them in Europe also, at least, and probably other places.

427
00:29:15,500 --> 00:29:23,000
Rym: It's not the kind of pumpkin, especially because we have cultivated them over the years to be big and large and suitable for decoration and carving.

428
00:29:23,260 --> 00:29:29,840
Scott: Oh yeah, well people try to grow, like they always had these competitions, like biggest pumpkin, because they've been breeding them so long to be that way.

429
00:29:29,880 --> 00:29:34,060
Rym: There are way better squash and way better pumpkins to eat than the ones that you carve.

430
00:29:34,700 --> 00:29:49,480
Rym: But, that said, a family tradition that I do miss and should do again at some point, when we were kids, we'd get pumpkins for carving, we'd scoop all the guts out, but then we'd take all the seeds, clean them, dry them, roast them in the oven, and then have pumpkin seeds for the next few months.

431
00:29:50,200 --> 00:29:51,620
Scott: Yeah, pumpkin seeds are, they're good.

432
00:29:54,007 --> 00:29:55,059
Scott: Get a little salt on there.

433
00:29:57,281 --> 00:30:06,440
Rym: But pumpkin carving loomed really large in my childhood life, I think just partly because holidays loom really large in your life as a kid, especially in America.

434
00:30:07,280 --> 00:30:17,420
Scott: Yeah, I can't remember average day of elementary school, but I can remember birthday, Halloween, holiday kind of things better, at least.

435
00:30:17,801 --> 00:30:21,669
Rym: And Halloween was, at least in my life, a really big deal.

436
00:30:21,689 --> 00:30:26,119
Rym: I think it's actually, for a lot of kids, for a lot of reasons, less of a big deal now than it used to be.

437
00:30:27,020 --> 00:30:37,296
Rym: But, like, every kid dressed up, a lot of the adults, the teachers would dress up, we'd go trick or treating all night, and it seemed like everyone was out.

438
00:30:37,336 --> 00:30:39,540
Rym: trick or treating, like trick or treating was a huge deal.

439
00:30:40,180 --> 00:30:43,788
Rym: Everyone had pumpkins, we'd all be talking about the pumpkins we carved.

440
00:30:44,028 --> 00:30:49,520
Rym: Some kids would, like, bring their pumpkin to school to show it off, like, it just was like a whole thing.

441
00:30:50,160 --> 00:30:56,675
Rym: And for as much as I cared about carving pumpkins, personally, I was dogshit at it.

442
00:30:56,775 --> 00:30:58,940
Rym: I was so bad at it, my pumpkins looked like crap.

443
00:30:59,740 --> 00:31:08,000
Scott: Well, I think we were not, you know, we didn't have any example to go on other than, you know, our neighbors, and it's not like people were sharing photos or anything.

444
00:31:08,441 --> 00:31:18,440
Scott: It's like, you didn't, I didn't even know what, like, my family, other, you know, like, close relatives who didn't live nearby, I didn't know what their pumpkins looked like because they were far away, right?

445
00:31:18,500 --> 00:31:20,122
Scott: I didn't go to them on Halloween.

446
00:31:20,142 --> 00:31:21,223
Scott: Halloween was a local deal.

447
00:31:21,243 --> 00:31:36,800
Scott: So you only knew what pumpkins looked like in your neighborhood, and you knew what they looked like on TV, and you could, I guess, who was smart enough to, like, go to the library and get a book on it, but would there even be such a thing, and what kind of information would it have about more advanced techniques?

448
00:31:37,301 --> 00:31:43,589
Scott: It's like, unless you were, like, a special, like, a harvest engraver or something, how would you know to do advanced pumpkin shit?

449
00:31:43,609 --> 00:31:47,053
Scott: So for a lot of our life, pumpkin carving was real simple.

450
00:31:47,113 --> 00:31:49,075
Scott: Triangle eyeball, right?

451
00:31:49,095 --> 00:31:52,740
Scott: You know, crescent mouth, maybe with a swaggle tooth or two in there, right?

452
00:31:53,101 --> 00:31:58,920
Rym: Usually I'd try to make the little square tooth coming out, but if I messed it up, I'd cut it off and put pumpkin seeds in there as teeth.

453
00:31:59,823 --> 00:32:00,719
Scott: Oh, that's a bad idea.

454
00:32:01,443 --> 00:32:02,240
Rym: That was the backup plan.

455
00:32:02,260 --> 00:32:10,418
Scott: I usually successfully got one or two square teeth on there in the crescent mouth, but generally it's just, yeah, you cut a hole in the top, all right?

456
00:32:10,498 --> 00:32:11,380
Scott: You take the stem off.

457
00:32:12,140 --> 00:32:17,870
Scott: Even though I've learned more recently that actually, thanks to the internet, right, the exact opposite of what I just told you, right?

458
00:32:17,910 --> 00:32:19,953
Scott: With the internet, I can learn more advanced techniques.

459
00:32:20,454 --> 00:32:23,980
Scott: I learned that it's probably better to cut a hole in the bottom, that a pumpkin will last longer that way.

460
00:32:25,421 --> 00:32:30,856
Scott: You cut the hole in the bottom, if you're smart and you've read the internet, you scoop out all the shit, right?

461
00:32:30,996 --> 00:32:32,160
Scott: Cause you got to put the candle in there.

462
00:32:33,322 --> 00:32:36,348
Scott: Or I guess nowadays you could put an LED light in there, probably be better.

463
00:32:36,448 --> 00:32:38,613
Scott: Get some color changing going on, right?

464
00:32:38,734 --> 00:32:39,676
Scott: Don't have to worry about fire.

465
00:32:39,696 --> 00:32:41,299
Scott: It stays on all the time.

466
00:32:43,060 --> 00:32:44,403
Scott: You get a few of them in there.

467
00:32:44,423 --> 00:32:51,360
Scott: You get different ones in the eyes or something, you know, and then you have the skills of an artist, you draw on the front of it.

468
00:32:52,281 --> 00:33:00,520
Scott: And then you take your special knife that you got from the pumpkin carving kit and you poke it in there and you saw out the area that you drew on.

469
00:33:01,241 --> 00:33:12,900
Scott: Or if you're even fancier, you could do an etching basically where you scrape away the pumpkin surface and then you scrape deeper in areas you want more light to shine through or even go all the way through.

470
00:33:13,923 --> 00:33:15,497
Rym: That's like the advanced technique.

471
00:33:15,980 --> 00:33:31,440
Scott: That's the most advanced technique and in other areas you only scrape away a little bit and then you can sort of have a scale of brightness of the light shining through and really get some high resolution artworks on there.

472
00:33:32,241 --> 00:33:33,563
Rym: Okay, I put it on the stream.

473
00:33:33,603 --> 00:33:37,948
Rym: I found a picture of the, there are way better pumpkin carving tools available today.

474
00:33:37,988 --> 00:33:45,498
Rym: When we were kids, you basically, if you even had a pumpkin carving kit, you probably had a poker, which is basically just a less sharp owl, AWL.

475
00:33:47,561 --> 00:33:58,935
Rym: You had the shittiest, like, dullest butter knife saw with ridges on both sides that you'd use to like cut the thing and then a scoop that broke immediately.

476
00:33:58,975 --> 00:34:03,360
Rym: and then you just use one of your parents' like kitchen scoops or kitchen spoons instead.

477
00:34:03,860 --> 00:34:11,998
Rym: And if you were real lucky, you also had a little thing you could stick into the bottom of the pumpkin to hold up the candles so it would be a little higher to get more light out.

478
00:34:13,741 --> 00:34:18,027
Rym: It was like this plastic kit that was on sale at every store and everyone I knew had the exact same kit.

479
00:34:18,047 --> 00:34:18,728
Rym: They were all red.

480
00:34:19,050 --> 00:34:25,179
Rym: Like they were red, the cheapest plastic, like we're talking Bakelite levels of crap plastic and they cost like a dollar.

481
00:34:28,420 --> 00:34:32,204
Rym: Yeah, our technique before the internet, like, I'm going to ignore modern pumpkin carving.

482
00:34:33,024 --> 00:34:41,431
Rym: As a kid, the technique our family used was basically just like the family had a, I don't know, communal passed down technique.

483
00:34:41,491 --> 00:34:42,992
Rym: Like the family kind of had a way.

484
00:34:43,052 --> 00:34:53,920
Rym: we did it, but we'd always put newspaper all over the floor in the kitchen and we'd have to lock the dogs outside because the dogs were a problem when the pumpkins were being carved for all the reasons you can imagine.

485
00:34:54,719 --> 00:35:08,573
Rym: And we'd make the biggest mess, but we'd basically carve the top out, scoop the guts out, and then we'd try to dry the pumpkin out a little bit under the idea that if we dried it out a little bit in the oven, like on low, that would make, that would have less moisture.

486
00:35:08,933 --> 00:35:14,038
Rym: So it'd be less likely to mold and get disgusting too early and thus would last longer.

487
00:35:14,498 --> 00:35:16,520
Rym: I don't think that actually worked, but that's what we did.

488
00:35:17,382 --> 00:35:21,520
Scott: I think the hole in the bottom instead of the top based on my readings would make it last longer.

489
00:35:21,921 --> 00:35:30,040
Rym: I remember I used a thing of the day years and years ago where someone did an experiment where they tried like soaking up one pumpkin in bleach and like doing all these different techniques.

490
00:35:30,260 --> 00:35:31,319
Scott: That seems like it's not going to work.

491
00:35:31,680 --> 00:35:32,103
Rym: Yeah.

492
00:35:32,123 --> 00:35:35,060
Rym: And the conclusion at the end was nothing saves a pumpkin.

493
00:35:35,940 --> 00:35:36,541
Scott: Yeah, of course not.

494
00:35:36,601 --> 00:35:38,644
Scott: nothing saves it.

495
00:35:38,905 --> 00:35:40,527
Scott: Every year I get a pumpkin these days.

496
00:35:40,547 --> 00:35:48,920
Scott: I usually buy a medium sized one and I put it out on the balcony in early October and then I forget to throw it away until March or so.

497
00:35:50,305 --> 00:35:50,919
Rym: That's the way.

498
00:35:51,780 --> 00:35:51,961
Scott: Yeah.

499
00:35:52,121 --> 00:35:55,006
Scott: And that pumpkin, that pumpkin, I don't cut it open.

500
00:35:55,026 --> 00:35:56,429
Scott: I don't carve it or anything.

501
00:35:56,489 --> 00:35:58,733
Scott: It's just a medium sized, you know, decorative guy.

502
00:35:58,753 --> 00:36:01,398
Scott: And it usually costs like a dollar or whatever.

503
00:36:01,758 --> 00:36:02,540
Scott: Five dollars maybe.

504
00:36:02,800 --> 00:36:12,220
Rym: We made a big deal about picking our pumpkins that we'd always go to an apple picking place, pick some apples, go through our corn maze because those were, those were every place on a corn maze.

505
00:36:12,681 --> 00:36:18,991
Rym: And then at the end of the day we would go and I am confident our parents hated this aspect.

506
00:36:19,772 --> 00:36:24,500
Rym: We would spend like over an hour trying to find the exact perfect pumpkins.

507
00:36:25,761 --> 00:36:29,840
Scott: I mean, we obviously just didn't take any old pumpkin, but it didn't take us that long.

508
00:36:30,261 --> 00:36:33,786
Rym: We were like weird about it, partly because like we both wanted to have the biggest one.

509
00:36:33,906 --> 00:36:35,769
Rym: I liked very tall pumpkins.

510
00:36:36,270 --> 00:36:43,120
Rym: Like I wanted a very Dan Aykroyd style pumpkin while my brother usually wanted a more like John Belushi, big wide pumpkin.

511
00:36:45,720 --> 00:36:45,981
Scott: Okay.

512
00:36:46,381 --> 00:36:58,140
Scott: Yeah, we just, you know, but I'm talking about nowadays I get the medium sized guy and I put him out there and even though it's not opened at all, it does, you know, go bad.

513
00:37:01,123 --> 00:37:05,300
Scott: So if you cut it open, oh yeah, you just cut the life of that by a ton.

514
00:37:06,220 --> 00:37:09,988
Rym: We'd also have the problem of we'd, we'd kinda, and other people in the neighborhood would do this too.

515
00:37:10,028 --> 00:37:15,580
Rym: A lot of families would leave the kids pumpkins out kind of as long as possible on the front stoop.

516
00:37:16,060 --> 00:37:19,189
Rym: Like it's almost like every kid was like, oh, my pumpkin made it the longest.

517
00:37:19,850 --> 00:37:23,420
Rym: And usually they got scootled when they started to turn into slime.

518
00:37:25,521 --> 00:37:32,940
Rym: I definitely had one turned into slime one year and insisted that my parents leave it out even after it turned into slime and then a bunch of squirrels ate it and it disappeared.

519
00:37:33,980 --> 00:37:34,080
Scott: Yeah.

520
00:37:34,100 --> 00:37:39,250
Scott: I mean, yeah, it was much, you know, nowadays it's like I said, the pumpkin sits on the balcony till March or whatever.

521
00:37:39,351 --> 00:37:44,120
Scott: And the reason is because to throw it out, I have to go out there in the cold, get it, bring it inside.

522
00:37:44,961 --> 00:37:47,387
Scott: It's like, it's like a little bit of work to go get rid of it.

523
00:37:47,788 --> 00:37:52,280
Scott: Whereas when we were in a house, it's like it was sitting on the porch.

524
00:37:53,520 --> 00:38:00,351
Scott: So it's like, so it's like, so it's like I go outside and if I see it there and it's November and it's getting, it's not looking good.

525
00:38:00,472 --> 00:38:02,295
Scott: I just did the trash cans right there.

526
00:38:02,355 --> 00:38:02,896
Scott: Just like, bye.

527
00:38:04,038 --> 00:38:05,520
Scott: So that was never an issue.

528
00:38:05,680 --> 00:38:06,641
Rym: The listener has a question.

529
00:38:06,701 --> 00:38:07,743
Rym: What about white pumpkins?

530
00:38:08,363 --> 00:38:09,885
Rym: I have an opinion about those.

531
00:38:10,686 --> 00:38:11,267
Rym: I was a kid.

532
00:38:11,867 --> 00:38:19,797
Rym: The only houses that had a white pumpkin that they carved were, and I'm going to characterize them broadly, but this was my perception as a kid and I think it holds up today.

533
00:38:20,397 --> 00:38:22,660
Rym: They were the extremely yuppie families.

534
00:38:23,760 --> 00:38:37,337
Scott: No, I mean, I never saw anyone make, there's no reason you couldn't make a jack-o-lantern out of another variety of pumpkin, but I never seen anyone not use the normal orange one because that's widely available.

535
00:38:37,357 --> 00:38:39,460
Scott: You'd have to really go out of your way to get something different.

536
00:38:39,720 --> 00:38:40,783
Rym: The orchards had the white pumpkins.

537
00:38:40,843 --> 00:38:44,615
Scott: I remember seeing them there, but yeah, but those are always in like the eating gourd section.

538
00:38:44,655 --> 00:38:46,240
Scott: They weren't in the carving gourd section.

539
00:38:51,801 --> 00:38:59,449
Rym: The houses that had the white pumpkins carved were the same houses that when they put up Christmas lights, they only put up white Christmas lights.

540
00:38:59,489 --> 00:39:02,512
Rym: They didn't put up like the multicolored mess that everyone else did.

541
00:39:02,552 --> 00:39:09,400
Rym: They'd have like all white meticulously designed and then their pumpkins were white and they usually had those synthetic non-green Christmas trees.

542
00:39:10,081 --> 00:39:16,132
Scott: Yeah, I mean, getting a different colored gourd, if possible, it could be good based on what you want to carve on there, right?

543
00:39:16,152 --> 00:39:20,580
Scott: Maybe you want to do something ghosty, a white one might be better and more suitable for your artwork.

544
00:39:22,661 --> 00:39:25,528
Rym: Yeah, I have no prejudice against non-orange pumpkins today.

545
00:39:25,649 --> 00:39:29,940
Rym: I'm just only talking from the perspective of like six-year-old Rym living in Detroit.

546
00:39:30,940 --> 00:39:35,767
Scott: Yeah, I mean, you know, maybe someone could even get creative, get like, you know, some yellow squashes in there.

547
00:39:35,787 --> 00:39:41,374
Scott: I don't know how you're going to carve it, but there's not a lot of room in there for the candle, but maybe you could pull it off, right?

548
00:39:42,115 --> 00:39:43,517
Scott: There is a space inside.

549
00:39:45,039 --> 00:39:45,800
Scott: Good luck, I guess.

550
00:39:46,140 --> 00:39:47,900
Rym: I remember one year at RIT, I did buy...

551
00:39:48,760 --> 00:39:49,558
Scott: Probably better to eat it.

552
00:39:50,080 --> 00:39:55,100
Rym: I bought a mini pumpkin at Wegmans, like one of the little tiny ones, the little tiny orange one that's like four inches across.

553
00:39:55,782 --> 00:39:59,735
Scott: Yeah, you can't really carve those, you just put them somewhere.

554
00:39:59,755 --> 00:40:00,758
Scott: That's like a desk decoration.

555
00:40:01,980 --> 00:40:09,620
Rym: I got an X-Acto knife and a tiny little saw, and I tried to make a tiny little jack-o-lantern, and I basically destroyed the pumpkin and then I had to throw it away.

556
00:40:10,240 --> 00:40:13,780
Scott: Yeah, those don't really have a cavity in them to make a jack-o-lantern from.

557
00:40:13,860 --> 00:40:16,191
Rym: Yeah, it was a disaster.

558
00:40:16,211 --> 00:40:17,859
Rym: I remember being mad about it.

559
00:40:18,442 --> 00:40:22,079
Scott: What you got to do with those is something you see, you know, is you got to paint on it.

560
00:40:24,003 --> 00:40:32,440
Scott: A lot of people will do that where they'll just, you know, pumpkins of all sizes, but usually the smaller ones that you can't cut open, they will paint something on there.

561
00:40:32,500 --> 00:40:41,200
Scott: And of course, well, the world's your oyster if you're painting on there, and then you got to cover that paint up with some kind of top coat to really seal it in and protect it from the elements.

562
00:40:41,882 --> 00:40:48,899
Scott: And the only problem with these is, you know, the paintings can be whatever, they can even be amazing, they can be crappy, you know.

563
00:40:50,040 --> 00:40:56,680
Scott: But you got to have some sort of external light to shine onto them, otherwise people come to Trick or Treat and they can't see that shit.

564
00:40:58,963 --> 00:40:59,944
Rym: Ah, Trick or Treat.

565
00:40:59,984 --> 00:41:08,440
Rym: The thing is, what I miss about that era was that we'd go Trick or Treating and we'd see everyone else's pumpkins and kind of comment on them.

566
00:41:08,500 --> 00:41:10,971
Rym: And we'd always talk about, like, which house had the best pumpkin?

567
00:41:11,011 --> 00:41:13,040
Rym: What was the best pumpkin we saw on the way?

568
00:41:13,801 --> 00:41:20,380
Rym: And I just read an article this morning talking about how in a lot of Connecticut towns, kids really don't Trick or Treat at all anymore.

569
00:41:21,140 --> 00:41:23,559
Scott: No, Trick or Treating has gone down a whole bunch, I think.

570
00:41:23,800 --> 00:41:24,618
Rym: For a lot of reasons.

571
00:41:25,541 --> 00:41:29,655
Scott: Yeah, because, you know, parents are afraid of danger, right?

572
00:41:29,695 --> 00:41:31,200
Scott: Who am I to say I'm not a parent?

573
00:41:31,380 --> 00:41:36,590
Scott: But statistically, shit was way more dangerous in the 50s and the 60s.

574
00:41:36,630 --> 00:41:41,860
Scott: There were way more, if you look at crime stats, per capita and such and such.

575
00:41:42,720 --> 00:41:51,440
Scott: People in the 50s and 60s, all these boomers who are afraid now, maybe they are afraid because things were so dangerous when they were kids, but their parents let them roam free in that danger.

576
00:41:52,061 --> 00:41:54,785
Scott: And survivorship bias, they survived until now.

577
00:41:55,266 --> 00:42:03,380
Scott: And while the world is full of dangers, I just read, you know, it's like you can always turn on the evening news and find at least one story, right?

578
00:42:04,000 --> 00:42:07,668
Scott: But quantifiably, it's much safer now.

579
00:42:07,748 --> 00:42:13,380
Scott: You'd actually be better off letting your kids roam free today than statistically speaking.

580
00:42:13,480 --> 00:42:22,300
Rym: But not Trick or Treating, because actually the lead in that article was interesting and it ties back to my news earlier.

581
00:42:22,940 --> 00:42:25,464
Rym: The single biggest danger to kids Trick or Treating is getting hit by cars.

582
00:42:25,524 --> 00:42:32,636
Rym: And kids get hit by cars in such high quantity during Trick or Treating, that is the highest cited reason.

583
00:42:32,997 --> 00:42:34,840
Rym: parents have stopped taking their kids Trick or Treating.

584
00:42:35,160 --> 00:42:36,883
Rym: Not fear of poisoning the candy.

585
00:42:36,923 --> 00:42:39,106
Rym: That's only like the New York Post rag nonsense.

586
00:42:39,507 --> 00:42:41,009
Rym: Not like all those other reasons.

587
00:42:41,349 --> 00:42:44,314
Rym: A big part of it was kids got hit by cars all the time.

588
00:42:44,414 --> 00:42:45,275
Rym: It was a real danger.

589
00:42:45,295 --> 00:42:48,640
Rym: A lot of parents knew another parent who had a kid hit by a car.

590
00:42:49,643 --> 00:42:50,719
Scott: Yep, that's true.

591
00:42:51,160 --> 00:42:52,401
Rym: And think about it, it's a perfect storm.

592
00:42:52,662 --> 00:43:02,950
Rym: You've got kids going out at dusk, the most dangerous time to be around cars, in neighborhoods where there's basically no enforcement of traffic rules, especially subdivisions.

593
00:43:04,440 --> 00:43:08,686
Rym: And it's kids who are off roaming, running around in the street, wearing costumes, masks.

594
00:43:08,706 --> 00:43:09,928
Rym: they can't see out of that well.

595
00:43:10,349 --> 00:43:17,980
Rym: And also, statistically, Halloween is one of, it's not the highest, but it's in the top 10 drunk driving nights in America.

596
00:43:18,343 --> 00:43:20,640
Rym: More drunk drivers out on Halloween than many other nights.

597
00:43:20,880 --> 00:43:25,647
Scott: You'd hope though that the drunken people should be driving way later than the kids are Trick or Treating.

598
00:43:26,227 --> 00:43:29,652
Scott: If they're kids, they should be Trick or Treating like 6, 7, maybe 8.

599
00:43:29,652 --> 00:43:38,761
Scott: Whereas the drunken people you'd imagine are driving home 10, 11, 12, 1, 5.

600
00:43:38,761 --> 00:43:42,965
Scott: But yeah, it's like, you know, this is another reason to try to live in a place without cars.

601
00:43:43,505 --> 00:43:48,530
Scott: And then kids can, one of the many benefits is that kids can just go out Trick or Treating freely.

602
00:43:49,010 --> 00:43:59,580
Scott: Right now, in our neighborhood, right, the open street, which usually is only a weekend thing, on Halloween, they got the open street going on and there's all kinds of Halloween events happening there.

603
00:44:00,061 --> 00:44:04,267
Scott: Everyone bringing out their kids like, you know, we block off a space and we say no cars here.

604
00:44:04,828 --> 00:44:06,851
Scott: And then the Halloween happens in that space.

605
00:44:06,931 --> 00:44:12,360
Rym: Funny how every time you block off a street to cars, suddenly that street naturally fills with people doing things.

606
00:44:12,720 --> 00:44:25,839
Scott: I'm also happy I heard that there is a truck there with a haunted experience involving an alien and kids have, at least according to one report, are screaming and in tears after going in this truck.

607
00:44:25,859 --> 00:44:26,560
Scott: Oh that's fantastic.

608
00:44:27,401 --> 00:44:28,142
Scott: I love that shit.

609
00:44:28,602 --> 00:44:42,718
Scott: And also another thing that happens in our neighborhood, in the major city, is that kids will not Trick or Treat at houses very much because there aren't houses, there's apartment buildings and it's not like you can go in an apartment building.

610
00:44:42,738 --> 00:44:44,780
Scott: you don't live in and start knocking on all the apartments.

611
00:44:45,281 --> 00:44:46,983
Scott: But you can do it in your own building.

612
00:44:47,003 --> 00:44:50,888
Scott: If you live in an apartment building, very often kids will Trick or Treat in the building.

613
00:44:52,850 --> 00:45:00,200
Scott: But you can't really have a jack-o-lantern with a fire in the building at your door, but you can get one with the LEDs now.

614
00:45:00,541 --> 00:45:05,490
Scott: I saw some LED ones in the hallway.

615
00:45:06,552 --> 00:45:11,100
Scott: Back in the olden days, I guess people would just have fire in the hallway or not have jack-o-lanterns in the building.

616
00:45:11,301 --> 00:45:15,980
Rym: Back in the old days, people would have a Christmas tree covered with literal candles with actual fire.

617
00:45:17,060 --> 00:45:21,585
Scott: And they would have fire.

618
00:45:22,987 --> 00:45:25,209
Scott: Yeah, but that's how it is.

619
00:45:25,270 --> 00:45:25,790
Scott: Oh, that's right.

620
00:45:26,050 --> 00:45:34,600
Scott: The other thing kids do is they will go to a street that has a commercial street, like an avenue with a bunch of businesses, and they will Trick or Treat at the businesses.

621
00:45:34,760 --> 00:45:40,415
Scott: Because you never know what crazy you're going to find going up to a house or an apartment, but all the businesses, they're ready.

622
00:45:41,337 --> 00:45:42,460
Scott: They got candy for the children.

623
00:45:42,540 --> 00:45:43,826
Scott: Remember in case we did that one year?

624
00:45:43,866 --> 00:45:46,800
Rym: We went to Lakeside Mall, and the mall was open for Trick or Treat.

625
00:45:47,000 --> 00:45:49,165
Scott: Oh, I've seen Trick or Treating in malls before, yeah.

626
00:45:49,767 --> 00:45:52,152
Scott: But I just remember one time I was in a store.

627
00:45:52,453 --> 00:45:55,380
Scott: I was picking up a sandwich for dinner on Halloween or something.

628
00:45:55,880 --> 00:46:01,072
Scott: And some kid comes in and Trick or Treats while I'm waiting for my sandwich, and the dude gives an apple.

629
00:46:02,334 --> 00:46:04,820
Scott: And the kid was happy to get the apple.

630
00:46:05,680 --> 00:46:05,743
Rym: Wow.

631
00:46:07,561 --> 00:46:08,283
Scott: I'm like, "Damn.

632
00:46:08,584 --> 00:46:09,066
Scott: All right.

633
00:46:09,407 --> 00:46:09,748
Scott: How about

634
00:46:09,768 --> 00:46:10,049
Scott: that?".

635
00:46:11,353 --> 00:46:12,657
Scott: But also, "Dude, give it an

636
00:46:12,778 --> 00:46:13,039
Scott: apple.".

637
00:46:13,541 --> 00:46:15,060
Rym: There was one old lady in our neighborhood.

638
00:46:15,060 --> 00:46:17,940
Scott: Well, it's like I was waiting for Radish Skull to come in next.

639
00:46:18,240 --> 00:46:21,505
Rym: When we were kids, there was one old lady in our neighborhood who was super sweet.

640
00:46:21,986 --> 00:46:30,320
Rym: And on Halloween, she made traditional, old-school candied apples on the sticks for real.

641
00:46:31,121 --> 00:46:31,779
Scott: She made them.

642
00:46:32,563 --> 00:46:33,651
Rym: She made them.

643
00:46:33,771 --> 00:46:34,939
Rym: Hundreds of them.

644
00:46:37,241 --> 00:46:39,987
Scott: During Trick or Treating, I've never had anyone give anything that was made.

645
00:46:40,007 --> 00:46:45,700
Scott: It was always purchased food, candies, even if they were the shitty ones.

646
00:46:46,100 --> 00:46:48,386
Rym: Yeah.

647
00:46:48,887 --> 00:46:50,992
Rym: But I sadly didn't carve a pumpkin this year.

648
00:46:51,012 --> 00:46:51,994
Rym: I've probably just been too busy.

649
00:46:53,457 --> 00:46:57,973
Scott: This has been just generally- Where am I gonna put- If I carve a pumpkin, it's gonna sit on the balcony where no one will see it.

650
00:46:58,033 --> 00:46:59,920
Scott: So I just put the pumpkin out there uncarved.

651
00:46:59,960 --> 00:47:04,400
Rym: Well, our balcony faces basically a garden surrounded by all these other apartment buildings.

652
00:47:06,980 --> 00:47:10,249
Scott: Maybe somebody with binoculars or a telescope will see it from afar.

653
00:47:11,712 --> 00:47:13,717
Scott: Zoom in, like, "Oh, I see a pumpkin in the distance.

654
00:47:13,757 --> 00:47:14,259
Scott: Let me check it

655
00:47:14,399 --> 00:47:14,499
Scott: out.".

656
00:47:14,821 --> 00:47:16,097
Rym: I mean, those balconies are all close enough.

657
00:47:18,925 --> 00:47:20,520
Scott: Or a butthole or a goat's-y or something.

658
00:47:24,081 --> 00:47:26,427
Rym: I always enjoyed carving pumpkins, even though I was bad at it.

659
00:47:26,608 --> 00:47:31,160
Rym: That's the lesson I really take away from this, is that you don't have to be good at something to enjoy doing it.

660
00:47:31,260 --> 00:47:32,734
Rym: I mean, look, we're doing GeekNights for 19 years.

661
00:47:34,221 --> 00:47:45,999
Scott: I think the lesson is that it's like, "Hey, we've managed to keep a tradition that requires people to do some kind of arts and crafts, even if they don't do that at any other point in their

662
00:47:46,039 --> 00:47:46,340
Scott: lives.".

663
00:47:47,740 --> 00:47:56,260
Scott: They've pushed arts and crafts out of their lives after they left school or summer camp or wherever the last time they did an art or a craft, if they're not a crafty person.

664
00:47:57,101 --> 00:48:07,080
Scott: And yet, if they have children or celebrate Halloween in some way, the arts and crafts still comes and gets them once a year.

665
00:48:08,660 --> 00:48:24,360
Rym: I mean, even walking out to get lunch today in midtown Manhattan, I walked past one apartment building that's obviously a rich person effectively house in midtown, and they had a little skeleton decoration, and they had a very well-carved traditional jack-o-lantern with a candle in it.

666
00:48:24,740 --> 00:48:26,059
Scott: They paid someone to do that.

667
00:48:26,360 --> 00:48:26,584
Rym: Maybe.

668
00:48:26,645 --> 00:48:27,520
Rym: Maybe they made it themselves.

669
00:48:28,901 --> 00:48:34,234
Rym: It's that place that had a bunch of climate change is real decorations a couple years ago.

670
00:48:34,254 --> 00:48:36,239
Rym: I think I took a picture of it.

671
00:48:39,341 --> 00:48:40,689
Rym: That's all I got.

672
00:48:40,689 --> 00:48:42,137
Rym: 19 years of GeekNights.

673
00:48:42,358 --> 00:48:42,740
Rym: God damn.

674
00:48:45,822 --> 00:48:48,260
Rym: So give us two weeks for everything to happen.

675
00:48:49,581 --> 00:48:53,253
Rym: Make a plan to vote, even if you think, "Oh, I'll vote on election

676
00:48:53,273 --> 00:48:53,714
Rym: day.".

677
00:48:54,156 --> 00:48:55,360
Rym: Make a specific plan.

678
00:48:56,980 --> 00:48:58,184
Scott: The plan's not complicated.

679
00:48:58,244 --> 00:49:01,997
Scott: It's like, "I'm going to wake up and immediately go to the polling place.

680
00:49:02,017 --> 00:49:02,519
Scott: This is where it

681
00:49:02,619 --> 00:49:02,680
Scott: is.".

682
00:49:02,820 --> 00:49:03,408
Rym: Yep.

683
00:49:03,448 --> 00:49:04,219
Rym: There's real data.

684
00:49:04,840 --> 00:49:06,169
Scott: "I'm going to go after work.

685
00:49:06,310 --> 00:49:07,115
Scott: It's on my calendar.

686
00:49:07,135 --> 00:49:07,578
Scott: Go after

687
00:49:07,598 --> 00:49:07,820
Scott: work.".

688
00:49:07,860 --> 00:49:13,898
Rym: If you make a specific plan, you're more likely to actually do it and less likely for something to get in your way and prevent you from doing it.

689
00:49:16,340 --> 00:49:25,780
Rym: Otherwise, we'll see you in a couple weeks, hopefully get back out of our business, and mark my words, we're going to do something for the 20th anniversary episode of GeekNights.

690
00:49:27,201 --> 00:49:32,657
Rym: I'm going to make a plan, even if it is us doing a live event at a venue that no one else comes to.

691
00:49:32,697 --> 00:49:33,720
Rym: That would be really funny.

692
00:49:34,420 --> 00:49:35,780
Scott: It could be a tiny, tiny venue.

693
00:49:35,900 --> 00:49:36,879
Rym: It could be a really tiny venue.

694
00:49:37,120 --> 00:49:38,780
Scott: There are some in the neighborhood we could use.

695
00:49:40,562 --> 00:49:43,296
Rym: The words I said were a venue.

696
00:49:43,417 --> 00:49:44,000
Rym: The word venue.

697
00:49:44,941 --> 00:49:46,142
Scott: I do know some venues.

698
00:49:46,162 --> 00:49:48,265
Scott: There's this Grove place and this QED place.

699
00:49:48,325 --> 00:49:50,988
Scott: I could go to them and be like, "Look, I got this podcast.

700
00:49:51,008 --> 00:49:53,490
Scott: We've got no listeners, but it's 20 years old.

701
00:49:53,530 --> 00:49:54,912
Scott: We've been doing this shit 20 years.

702
00:49:55,393 --> 00:49:56,674
Scott: We want to do one event.

703
00:49:56,694 --> 00:49:58,256
Scott: You've got a tiny little venue here.

704
00:49:59,077 --> 00:50:00,378
Scott: Let us just do this one

705
00:50:00,478 --> 00:50:00,598
Scott: thing.".

706
00:50:02,160 --> 00:50:03,404
Scott: It doesn't have to be on a Halloween.

707
00:50:03,424 --> 00:50:05,993
Scott: I know you've probably got something more important booked on Halloween.

708
00:50:06,013 --> 00:50:08,320
Scott: It could be day before, whatever.

709
00:50:09,942 --> 00:50:10,906
Scott: It might just be us two.

710
00:50:10,966 --> 00:50:11,889
Scott: We might not sell any tickets.

711
00:50:11,909 --> 00:50:12,832
Rym: Just let this happen.

712
00:50:20,580 --> 00:50:22,949
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

713
00:50:23,010 --> 00:50:25,540
Rym: Special thanks to DJ Pretzel for the opening theme.

714
00:50:25,740 --> 00:50:31,760
Scott: Be sure to visit our website at FrontRowClue.com for show notes, discussion, links, and more.

715
00:50:32,280 --> 00:50:34,905
Rym: Remember, GeekNights is not one, but four different shows.

716
00:50:35,205 --> 00:50:36,868
Rym: Mondays are science and technology.

717
00:50:36,908 --> 00:50:38,672
Rym: Tuesdays, gaming and games.

718
00:50:38,792 --> 00:50:40,595
Rym: Wednesdays are animation and comics.

719
00:50:40,916 --> 00:50:43,280
Rym: Thursdays are arbitrary and indiscriminate.

720
00:50:43,380 --> 00:50:48,980
Rym: GeekNights is distributed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License.

721
00:50:49,460 --> 00:50:53,790
Scott: GeekNights is recorded live with no studio and no audience.

722
00:50:54,272 --> 00:50:57,740
Scott: But unlike those other late shows, it's actually recorded at night.

723
00:50:58,362 --> 00:51:20,960
Rym: And the patron patrons on the 19th anniversary episode of GeekNights are... Alan Joyce, Lin Kiji, Dread Lily Tenebrae, Case Care Makes Music, Chris Addad, Christopher Orr, Clinton Walton, Finn, Joel Hays, Penny Rymer, Rebecca Dunn, Sam Erickson, Shervin Von Horl, and a bunch of people whose names I will not say.

724
00:51:22,241 --> 00:51:24,165
Rym: We will do something for the 20th year.

725
00:51:24,185 --> 00:51:27,131
Rym: I've actually got some venues in mind.

726
00:51:27,451 --> 00:51:31,620
Rym: Scott seems more interested in the idea than I expected, so watch out.

727
00:51:31,900 --> 00:51:38,040
Rym: But we're not going to announce anything about that until at least, like, August next year, so don't hold your breath.

